\begin_layout Section*
Problem 16.A1. CES trade, market clearing and the limits of a fixed point argument
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Restored from the theory problem in the SFU Fall 2024 final examination. The original four stage argument and CES weighting are retained. The symmetry and fixed point claims are corrected. The following timing and points are for practice, not the original exam allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suggested time: 70 minutes. Total: 60 points for Part I.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part I. By hand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are \begin_inset Formula $N\geq2$\end_inset
 countries, indexed by \begin_inset Formula $i$\end_inset
 for origins and \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 for destinations. Country \begin_inset Formula $i$\end_inset
 owns a fixed quantity \begin_inset Formula $Q_i>0$\end_inset
 of its unique variety, has no other goods endowment, and has factory price \begin_inset Formula $p_i>0$\end_inset
. Its income is \begin_inset Formula $y_i=p_iQ_i$\end_inset
. All varieties are traded competitively. Delivering one unit from \begin_inset Formula $i$\end_inset
 to \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 uses \begin_inset Formula $t_{ij}\geq1$\end_inset
 origin units, with \begin_inset Formula $t_{ii}=1$\end_inset
. Delivered prices are \begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}=p_it_{ij}$\end_inset
. There are no outside transfers or trade deficits. Destination \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 chooses nonnegative delivered quantities \begin_inset Formula $c_{ij}$\end_inset
 with budget \begin_inset Formula $\sum_i p_it_{ij}c_{ij}\leq y_j$\end_inset
 and utility \begin_inset Formula $U_j(c)=[\sum_i\beta_i^{(1-\sigma)/\sigma}c_{ij}^{(\sigma-1)/\sigma}]^{\sigma/(\sigma-1)}$\end_inset
, where \begin_inset Formula $\beta_i>0$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $\sigma>0$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $\sigma\ne1$\end_inset
. For \begin_inset Formula $0<\sigma<1$\end_inset
, define utility to be zero when any component is zero, its continuous boundary value. You may use the standard concavity of this CES aggregator. Bilateral expenditure is \begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}=p_it_{ij}c_{ij}$\end_inset
, including domestic expenditure when \begin_inset Formula $i=j$\end_inset
. Let \begin_inset Formula $Y=\sum_i y_i$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $\theta_i=y_i/Y$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Derive the optimal bilateral quantities, bilateral expenditures and destination unit expenditure index \begin_inset Formula $P_j$\end_inset
, step by step. Explain why the optimum exhausts the budget, is interior and is globally optimal. Your argument must preserve the displayed \begin_inset Formula $\beta_i$\end_inset
 weighting. [16]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) State physical market clearing, including iceberg use, and its nominal form. Use your expenditure formula to isolate \begin_inset Formula $\beta_ip_i$\end_inset
 as a function of \begin_inset Formula $y_i$\end_inset
, the trade costs and the destination indices. Explain why the income identity \begin_inset Formula $y_i=p_iQ_i$\end_inset
 must still hold when interpreting the result as the original fixed supply economy. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) Define outward multilateral resistance by \begin_inset Formula $\Pi_i^{1-\sigma}=\sum_j\theta_j(t_{ij}/P_j)^{1-\sigma}$\end_inset
. Eliminate \begin_inset Formula $\beta_ip_i$\end_inset
 to derive the structural gravity equation and the inward resistance system. Show the reciprocal scale freedom of the two resistance systems and state a normalization. Take logs to identify the exporter effect, importer effect and coefficient on \begin_inset Formula $\log t_{ij}$\end_inset
. Distinguish the conditional index normalization from changing nominal currency units in the underlying economy. [16]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(d) Suppose \begin_inset Formula $t_{ij}=t_{ji}$\end_inset
. You may use the following result without proof: for a strictly positive finite trade cost matrix and positive shares summing to one, the joint inward and outward resistance system has a positive index pair, and any two such pairs differ only by reciprocal scalar rescaling. Use this result to explain when a normalization with \begin_inset Formula $\Pi_i=P_i$\end_inset
 is available, and why symmetry alone does not make every unnormalized pair equal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the rest of this part use exactly the original diagnostic values \begin_inset Formula $N=2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1/2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $t_{ij}=1$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $\theta_i=1/2$\end_inset
. After choosing the symmetric normalization, write the inward equations as \begin_inset Formula $P=g(P)$\end_inset
. Determine whether \begin_inset Formula $g$\end_inset
 maps \begin_inset Formula $\{P:P_i\geq1\}$\end_inset
 into itself. Then use \begin_inset Formula $K=[1/2,2]^2$\end_inset
: prove that \begin_inset Formula $g$\end_inset
 maps \begin_inset Formula $K$\end_inset
 into itself and apply Brouwer to establish a fixed point. Determine all positive fixed points directly. Compute \begin_inset Formula $Dg$\end_inset
 at \begin_inset Formula $(1,1)$\end_inset
 and decide whether an induced matrix norm can be strictly below one there. Finally calculate two iterations from \begin_inset Formula $P^{(0)}=(2,2)$\end_inset
 and explain what they show about repeated substitution, existence and uniqueness. [18]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part II. Codex and verification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Construct a synthetic economy with at least ten countries, positive \begin_inset Formula $\beta_i,Q_i$\end_inset
, finite iceberg costs and \begin_inset Formula $\sigma>1$\end_inset
. Keep fixed physical supplies rather than silently treating both factory prices and nominal incomes as independent data. Solve for positive factory prices in log variables with one numeraire normalization. Compute incomes, delivered consumption, expenditure, both resistance systems and the physical shipment totals. Check every consumer budget, both row and column expenditure sums, all physical goods markets, positivity and the omitted redundant equation. Repeat under a change of currency units and verify how prices, incomes, nominal trade and physical quantities transform. Compare a normalized resistance iteration with a root solver conditional on the resulting income shares, align their index gauges before comparing them, and report full residuals as well as stopping criteria. For the synthetic fixed effect regression, require nonadditive bilateral variation in \begin_inset Formula $\log t_{ij}$\end_inset
 and report the design rank after choosing a dummy normalization. Recover the theoretical cost coefficient. Reproduce the two country diagnostic separately and do not infer convergence from an equation having a unique solution.
\end_layout

