\begin_layout Section*
Problem 16.A2. Arabasta and Baltigo: building the original gravity model of trade
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Restored from Problem 2 of the SFU December 2024 final examination. The original six modeling tasks, Arabasta and Baltigo, unequal country incomes, transport technology and distance comparisons are retained. Part II is a new computational extension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part I. By hand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suggested time: 75 minutes. Total: 60 points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two countries, Arabasta \begin_inset Formula $a$\end_inset
 and Baltigo \begin_inset Formula $b$\end_inset
, and one good from each origin. Quantity \begin_inset Formula $c_{ij}$\end_inset
 is consumption in destination \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 of origin \begin_inset Formula $i$\end_inset
's good. Nominal income and expenditure are \begin_inset Formula $y_a,y_b>0$\end_inset
. Origin prices are \begin_inset Formula $p_a,p_b>0$\end_inset
. Domestic delivery has no resource loss, while bilateral trade has the symmetric iceberg factor \begin_inset Formula $\tau\geq1$\end_inset
: \begin_inset Formula $p_{aa}=p_a$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p_{bb}=p_b$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p_{ab}=p_a\tau$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $p_{ba}=p_b\tau$\end_inset
. Delivering one unit abroad requires \begin_inset Formula $\tau$\end_inset
 units at origin. Bilateral expenditure is \begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}=p_{ij}c_{ij}$\end_inset
 and origin revenue clearing requires \begin_inset Formula $y_i=x_{ia}+x_{ib}$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use equal origin weights and common CES preferences. Write the substitution elasticity as \begin_inset Formula $\sigma>1$\end_inset
 and use \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=2$\end_inset
 for the closed form equilibrium calculations. The original key's power utility with exponent \begin_inset Formula $1/2$\end_inset
 is ordinally equivalent to this case; its exponent is not the substitution elasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Normalize \begin_inset Formula $p_b=1$\end_inset
. In Part I, \begin_inset Formula $y_a$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $y_b$\end_inset
 are fixed nominal incomes measured in these units. Origin supply quantities are not independently fixed: after solving the revenue equations they are implied by \begin_inset Formula $Q_i=y_i/p_i$\end_inset
. State this conditional nominal income closure when interpreting a counterfactual. It is not a comparison that holds both nominal incomes and physical endowments fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Original task 1.1. Specify a one parameter CES utility family and Arabasta's budget. Explain which goods, prices and income enter this problem. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Original task 1.2. Write the Lagrangian and derive both consumption quantities in Arabasta. Relabel carefully to obtain Baltigo's demands. Give a global optimality argument and state where your solution is interior. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) Original task 2.1. Write both countries' revenue clearing conditions. Specialize to \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=2$\end_inset
, impose \begin_inset Formula $p_b=1$\end_inset
, and solve explicitly for the unique positive origin price \begin_inset Formula $p_a$\end_inset
 as a function of \begin_inset Formula $y_a,y_b,\tau$\end_inset
. Reject any algebraic root that is not an admissible price, and check the case \begin_inset Formula $\tau=1$\end_inset
 directly. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(d) Original task 2.2. Let \begin_inset Formula $d\geq0$\end_inset
 be distance. Propose a one parameter engineering transport technology \begin_inset Formula $d=f(k)$\end_inset
 with nonincreasing returns in ship capital services \begin_inset Formula $k$\end_inset
. Normalize the rental cost of these services to one in the origin good resource units used to define iceberg shipping. Derive the per delivered unit resource requirement \begin_inset Formula $C(d)$\end_inset
 and set \begin_inset Formula $\tau(d)=1+C(d)$\end_inset
. Verify \begin_inset Formula $\tau(0)=1$\end_inset
 and that larger distance increases the barrier. Explain why you must not add a second monetary transport bill to the country budget. The historical key's constant returns choice is admissible; a concave power technology is also admissible. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(e) Original task 3.1. Eliminate the origin price from Arabasta's equilibrium import quantity \begin_inset Formula $c_{ba}$\end_inset
 and nominal imports \begin_inset Formula $x_{ba}$\end_inset
. Derive the corresponding exports \begin_inset Formula $c_{ab},x_{ab}$\end_inset
 and verify both budgets and both physical resource equations, including iceberg use. As a common numerical check, use \begin_inset Formula $y_a=5$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $y_b=2$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $d=1$\end_inset
 and the admissible technology \begin_inset Formula $f(k)=\sqrt{k}$\end_inset
. This check is part of the existing task, not an additional scored question. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(f) Original task 3.2. Prove that doubling both nominal incomes doubles bilateral nominal trade in this closure. Then prove that increasing distance strictly reduces bilateral nominal imports, holding those incomes fixed. Separate the response of import quantities from nominal import values, and explain why this is not yet a welfare theorem for a fixed physical endowment economy. [10]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part II. Codex and verification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a new extension, separate from the restored final question. Begin only after completing the hand arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1. Use the numerical seed from part (e), fix the engineering parameter, and vary only distance over \begin_inset Formula $d\geq0$\end_inset
. Ask Codex to implement your analytical price, expenditure and quantity formulas, and independently solve the two revenue equations. Compare both methods. Test positivity, budget exhaustion, balanced bilateral expenditure, the domestic expenditure residuals, and every origin resource equation. Include zero distance without evaluating a formula that divides by zero. Plot bilateral expenditure and both implied origin supply quantities. Explain explicitly which physical quantity changes when nominal GDP is held fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2. Optional different closure. Freeze physical endowments at the values you derived from the seed, and retain \begin_inset Formula $p_b=1$\end_inset
. Now income must be endogenous, \begin_inset Formula $y_a=p_aQ_a$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $y_b=Q_b$\end_inset
. Derive the positive price equation under this closure, establish uniqueness of its positive root, and solve it as distance varies. Verify the original consumer problems, physical market clearing and income accounting independently. Compare the two closures at the seed and away from it. Any utility comparison must use the same CES preference and the full fixed endowment closure, not interpret the fixed nominal GDP family as the same physical economy.
\end_layout

